<div>[Diagnosis]</div><div># Intracerebral hemorrhage (ICH), right parietal lobe with subdural hemoatoma, suspected cerebral amylod angiopathy related, onset on 2026/03/21, with left hemiparesis, left hemineglect, and cognitive impairment</div><div># Epileptic seizures</div><div># Type 2 diabetes mellitus</div><div># Atrial fibrillations, status post left atrial appendage closure on 2026/05/27</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>[Present illness]</div><div>This is a 82-year-old woman with the above medical history.</div><div><br></div><div>She was permorbidly a community-based ambulator with cane, ADLs were independent, and she could take a bath sitting. She had been in her usual health status until middle March, a<span style="background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;, &quot;Noto Color Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;">ccording to her daughters, she had experienced fall episodes after syncope with head injury on March 16 and March 18. There was no nausea, vomiting, consiousness disturbance, incontinence, or myoclonus. However, she was dependent on a diaper for toileting since then because they want to protect her from fall again and she requires contact guard for indoor walk.</span><span style="background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;, &quot;Noto Color Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;">&nbsp;However, on&nbsp;</span>March 21, 2026, sudden consiousness change was noted with non-stop myoclonus and incontinence reported by her daughters.&nbsp; According to the daughters, she could not follow command or move spontaneously at that time. She was sent to the TMUH emergency department.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>At ER triage, the consciousness was E1VTM1, and mild tachycardiac (BT 37.6'C, BP 117/84 mmHg, HR 131 bpm, RR 24 bpm, SpO2 100%). Brain CT showed right skull bone fracture, right parietal intracerebral hematoma with perifocal edema and adjacent subdural hematoma. Neurosurgeon was consulted and traumatic intracerebral hemorrhage with status epilepticus was impressed and she was admitted to the surgical intensive care unit for further management. She was dependent on mechanical ventilation, with one cardiac arrhythmias noted during weaning on March 24. She was managed with Keppra and blood pressure control. Repeated brain CTs on March 25 showed resolving hematoma in the right occipital lobe with decreased volume. She was then transferred to TSGH and then back to TMUH for LAA&nbsp;<span style="background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;, &quot;Noto Color Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;">closure</span>&nbsp;in the following months.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>Regarding neurological recovery, she regained consciousness and improved to E4VTM6 by March 26. Muscle strength improved to generally over 3. Functionally, she could roll and sit up with supervision, stand up with walker and minimal assistance, and walk with contact guard with walker with left leg slightly dragging. ADLs improved to modified independence with transfer, grooming, walking, dressing upper clothes, and continence. Toileting and bathing were still dependent. Cognitive impairment was noted with slight disorientation, attention deficit, impaired short term memory, as well as left hemineglect.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>[Family History:]&nbsp;</div><div>Father DM,HTN;&nbsp; Mother DM. DAUGHTERS HTN.</div><div><br></div><div>[Premorbid]</div><div>Living environment: 民生東路4F E+ with 老二老三&nbsp;</div><div>Occupation: 家管</div><div>Education: 高中</div><div>Caregiver: 巴氏 NOONA SINCE 4/18</div><div>Ambulation: communitywith cane</div><div>ADLs: independent</div><d